[Music]  it's in cities where most of the  information we have about black and  ethnically diverse people is gathered  but what if you live outside the big  cities  where you're hidden from the studies and  the surveys  louisa is one of nearly two million  black people who live in the uk  but one of only about a thousand living  in somerset  her experience is unlikely to be  captured in any study systemic racism is  obviously a massive problem and that  permeates  sort of all areas of society no matter  where we live but i think here in the  west country  i think we have a particular culture of  institutionalised racism  and within that culture racism doesn't  get challenged very much  and when people do challenge it or do  speak out they can be then abused or  victim blamed  and then sort of experience more  discrimination if you like  anecdotally racism is worse in rural  areas  that's certainly the experience of  somerset pastry chef waheed  no studies examine this but data shows  you're many more times likely to be  stopped and searched if you're black  living in the west country than in  london i've been told to  go back to where i come from a few times  i get lots of stairs from people  uh i've been racially abused  i'd say a couple of times obviously the  random stop in searches because  obviously there's not many people that  look like me out here so  um you get disproportionately stopped  yeah yeah it's happened to me about 12  or 13 times in my  lifetime here the different experiences  of people from ethnic communities living  in the countryside  is just one example of data that's  missing and if there are gaps in  knowledge  there will be gaps in policy which is  why the center on the dynamics of  ethnicity  is commissioning the biggest ever study  to find out  what's missing it will cover topics and  aspects of life  that are relatively unknown about for  minority communities  such as political attitudes financial  circumstances health and mental health  and well-being religious and ethnic  identities  there are some huge data gaps for the  last 10 years for instance the nhs  has not measured mental health and how  it intersects with ethnicity  but 10-year-old data suggests that black  people  are five times more likely to suffer a  severe  mental health illness than white people  white is held up as the norm  then you do grow up with a with you know  distorted sense of self and  have problems with identity and it's  definitely impacted by mental health  it may be subtle but cumulative the  things that impact  on why minority groups have different  outcomes  and experiences to the majority small  things such as  finding a slavery family memorialized  on the walls of your local church i came  to this beautiful church because i love  churches  and i found this memorial to azeri penny  and one of the things that said on it on  there was that he was benefactor to all  men and that made me feel quite upset  because i've done quite a lot of  research into the pinney family and i  know about their  slave trading activities plantation  owner john pinney said of slaves  surely god ordained them for use and  benefit for us  it's all in the past but many feel those  inequalities haven't fully  balanced out and only by knowing and  understanding the problem  can we try to solve it jason farrell sky  news

